Baba Yaga	An old witch of Russian fairy tales who travels in a mortar, steers with a pestle, and sweeps away her tracks with a birch broom. She lives in a hut that turns on chicken legs.
Bannik	The “bathhouse dweller,” a guardian spirit of the bathhouse in Russian folklore.
Bolotnik	A swamp-dwelling spirit or swamp demon.
Aleksandr Peresvet	A historical monk of the Trinity Lavra under Sergius of Radonezh, remembered as a warrior associated with the Battle of Kulikovo.
Chelubey|Temir-Murza	The champion of the Tatar side at the Battle of Kulikovo, called Chelubey by Russian chroniclers and Temir-Murza by his own countrymen.
Chernomor	An old wizard and sea-lord of Russian folklore whose name derives from “Black Sea.” In the fairy tale of Tsar Saltan, he and his thirty-three sons emerge from the sea to guard the island of the swan maiden.
Ded Grib	“Grandfather Mushroom,” a character inspired by a figure in the Soviet children’s film Morozko rather than by a historical source.
Dmitrii Donskoi|Dmitrii Ivanovich	A historical prince called Dmitrii Ivanovich in The Winternight Trilogy and remembered by the moniker “of the Don.”
Domovoi|Domovaya	The household guardian spirit of Russian folklore. Domovaya is the feminine form used for a witch’s household guardian; some traditions instead give the domovoi a wife called the kikimora.
Dvorovoi	The guardian spirit of the dvor, or dooryard, in Russian folklore.
Gamayun	A prophetic figure in Russian folklore, generally depicted as a black bird with a woman’s head.
Great Khan|Genghis Khan	Genghis Khan, whose descendants ruled the Rus’ through the Golden Horde.
Ivan	A figure from the fairy tale Marya Morevna whose brothers-in-law are bird-princes.
Leshy|Lesovik	A woodland spirit in Slavic mythology and protector of forests and animals.
Oleg of Ryazan|Oleg Ryazansky	The Grand Prince of Ryazan during the latter half of the fourteenth century, remembered for an ambiguous political role in the period surrounding the Battle of Kulikovo.
Poludnitsa|Lady Midday	Lady Midday, sister to Lady Midnight, who wanders the hayfields and causes heatstroke with her cutting shears.
Polunochnitsa|Lady Midnight	Literally “midnight woman,” a demon of folklore who emerges only at midnight and causes children’s nightmares. She is said to live in a swamp, and parents sang charms intended to send her back there.
Pozhar	A golden mare whose name means “fire” or “bonfire.”
Rusalka	A female water nymph of Russian folklore, likened in the glossary to a succubus.
Snegurochka|Snow-Maiden	The Snow-Maiden, a character who appears in several Russian fairy tales; her name derives from the Russian sneg, meaning “snow.”
Solovey	Vasya’s bay stallion. His name means “nightingale.”
Tuman	Sasha’s gray horse. His name means “mist.”
Upyr	The Slavic vampire, portrayed as more monstrous and less elegant than its Western European counterpart. The plural is upyry.
Vazila	The guardian of the stable and protector of livestock in Russian folklore.
Vladimir Andreevich|Vladimir the Bold	The best-known prince of Serpukhov, called “the Bold” for his courage. He was Dmitrii Donskoi’s first cousin and close adviser.
Vodianoy	A male water spirit of Russian folklore, often malicious.
Zima	Vasya’s filly. Her name means “winter.”
